{"name":"Chamois Leather","hex":"#ad8867","fetched":"2026-10-12","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"chamois leather","whole":true,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A soft suede leather, originally from the chamois but now made from sheepskin, used in cleaning and polishing."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/chamois_leather"}],"entries":[{"title":"Chamois leather","blocks":[{"text":"Chamois leather is a type of porous leather, traditionally the skin of the chamois, a type of European mountain goat, but today made almost exclusively from the flesh split of a sheepskin."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/26/Chamois-natural.jpg/330px-Chamois-natural.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Chamois_leather"},{"title":"Chamois","blocks":[{"text":"The chamois, or Alpine chamois, is a species of goat-antelope native to mountainous parts of Europe and Western Asia, from the Pyrenees and the Cantabrian Mountain Range, the Alps, the Apennines, the Dinarides, the Tatra to the Carpathian Mountains, the Balkan Mountains, the Rila–Rhodope massif, Pindus, the northeastern mountains of Turkey, and the Caucasus. It has also been introduced to the South Island of New Zealand. Some subspecies of chamois are strictly protected in the EU under the European Habitats Directive."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/f0/064_Wild_Chamois_Parc_r%C3%A9gional_Chasseral_Photo_by_Giles_Laurent.jpg/330px-064_Wild_Chamois_Parc_r%C3%A9gional_Chasseral_Photo_by_Giles_Laurent.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Chamois"},{"title":"Leather","blocks":[{"text":"Leather is a strong, flexible and durable material obtained from the tanning, or chemical treatment, of animal skins and hides to prevent decay. The most common leathers come from cattle, sheep, goats, equine animals, buffalo, pigs and 